findings.body:
The PR description says `comments.json` was refined with consistent 评论/名字 terms, but the head commit deletes the file. The diff shows `comments.json` with `deleted file mode` and 74 lines removed. Every comment-related string ("Add comment", "Reply", "Sort by", "Show comment", and so on) loses its Chinese translation.

Because `ghost/i18n/lib/i18n.js` configures `fallbackLng` to `'en'`, the failure is silent: Chinese readers get English comment UI, with no error.

::callout{type="warning"}
The description and the diff disagree. Either the wrong state was pushed, or the deletion is unintended. The comments.json content should be kept and refined, not removed.

::code{file="ghost/i18n/locales/zh/ghost.json" lines="3-8"}

The key `"{memberEmail} will no longer receive {newsletterName} newsletter."` used to translate to `"{memberEmail}将不会再收到{newsletterName}的新闻信。"`. The new value is `"{memberEmail}将不会再收到新闻信。"`, which drops `{newsletterName}`.

The recipient is told that an email address will stop receiving "the newsletter" but not which one. For a member subscribed to several newsletters, that is a real loss of information, and it was not needed for the terminology change.

::diff{file="ghost/i18n/locales/zh/portal.json" lines="9-9"}

The description says the zh copy now uses consistent "名字" terms. But `"Name"` is `名字` in ghost.json (line 31) and `名称` in portal.json (line 117). The portal change is a regression for this key, since the base had `名字` there.

The same English key therefore produces different Chinese labels depending on the surface. That makes the terminology pass look unfinished and is a maintenance trap for translators.

::code{file="ghost/i18n/locales/zh/portal.json" lines="117-117"}

`"You're not receiving emails"` was `"您当前不会收到电子邮件。"`, a present-tense status. It is now `"您将不会收到邮件。"`, which means "you will not receive emails", a future state.

This key is the short status label in the email-preferences UI, so the change alters what it reports. `"You're currently not receiving emails"` on the line above keeps the present-tense sense, so the two labels now read differently.

::diff{file="ghost/i18n/locales/zh/portal.json" lines="213-213"}

The PR aims for consistent terminology, but portal.json now uses both `邮箱` and `邮件` for the same concepts. Examples: `"Verify your email address is correct"` uses 邮箱地址 (line 199), `"Your email has failed to resubscribe"` uses 邮箱 (line 218), and `"You're not receiving emails"` uses 邮件 (line 213).

This is a readability nit, not a bug. It is worth aligning with the rest of the file while the file is being touched.

::code{file="ghost/i18n/locales/zh/portal.json" lines="199-218"}
